session-seam-workspace.txt -- can the workspace's session tests actually fail?
==============================================================================

    SFlowVizWorkspace.cpp  vs  FlowViz.UI.Workspace.SessionSeam
    killed 2   SURVIVED 1   INVALID 0   UNSCORED 0   skipped 0

The lone SURVIVED is the identity control, which is the verdict it must have.
Had it been killed, every other verdict on this page would be void -- a harness
that "catches" a comment is reacting to something other than the mutant.

WHAT WAS UNDER TEST
-------------------
`SFlowVizWorkspace::LoadState` applies a session to the view models and then
pushes the result into the bound volume component. Before task #64 the push did
not exist: a session load restored every panel and left the render showing the
PREVIOUS session. A screenshot of that bug looks entirely correct, because the
panels -- the part a human reads -- are right. That is why it warranted a test
rather than a glance.

PER-ARM ATTRIBUTION
-------------------
Attribution here is READ OFF THE LOGS, not derived. `Tools/run_tests.sh` writes
a timestamped backup of the engine log on every invocation, so the per-arm
detail survives even though `TEST_LOG` itself is overwritten each arm. (The
sibling campaign, session-workspace.README, could not do this and says so.)

The mapping does not rest on timestamps alone: each arm has a DISTINCT
pass-count signature, so the three runs identify themselves.

  baseline  13:21:20Z   2/2   pristine tree green under the filter
  arm 1     13:22:01Z   0/2   both tests fail            -> killed
  arm 2     13:22:35Z   1/2   ONLY MissingCase fails     -> killed
  arm 3     13:23:06Z   2/2   nothing fails              -> SURVIVED (control)

arm 1 -- "the load applies but never pushes"
    The `PushToVolume()` call is DELETED. Both tests fail, and they fail to the
    POISON values rather than to defaults:

        colour map      expected 2 (Inferno), was 4 (Turbo   = poison)
        value range max expected 11.75,       was -7.25      (poison)
        clip planes     expected 1,           was 0          (poison)

    That the observed values are the poison and not the defaults is the point.
    A fixture that poisons nothing cannot tell "the push did not happen" from
    "the push happened and copied a default" -- repo memory
    a-defaults-helper-can-supply-the-observed-value.

arm 2 -- "the push is guarded on success"
    `PushToVolume()` wrapped in `if (Applied.IsOk())`. This is the mutant worth
    the whole campaign, because it is the implementation a careful person would
    WRITE. It reads as prudence and is exactly backwards: a session whose case
    file has moved reports FileNotFound and still applies every panel, so the
    guard leaves the render stale in precisely the relink flow.

    SessionSeam PASSED under this mutant. Only SessionSeamMissingCase failed.
    The happy-path test alone would have certified the bug. That is the whole
    argument for the second test existing, and it is here as a measurement
    rather than as a claim.

arm 3 -- IDENTITY CONTROL
    A comment inserted above `SaveSession`. Must survive, and did. Its purpose
    is to make the other two verdicts mean something: without it, "killed" is
    consistent with a harness that fails on any edit at all.

WHY NO ARM USED `if (false)`
----------------------------
FlowVizRuntime builds with `-Werror` including `-Wunreachable-code`, so
`if (false) PushToVolume();` does not compile and would have scored INVALID --
not scored at all. Arm 4 of the sibling session-workspace campaign learned this
the expensive way. Arm 1 here is therefore a DELETION, and arm 2 uses a
condition the compiler cannot fold.

WHAT THIS CAMPAIGN DOES NOT COVER
---------------------------------
`SaveSession` is a one-line forward to the model and is exercised only as a
fixture step here -- the model-side save is covered by session-workspace.txt.
Nothing on this page speaks to the UI affordance: both entry points take a
FString path, and no menu item or file picker calls either (task #53). The
picker is deliberately absent -- this module has no DesktopPlatform dependency
and acquiring one would break packaged builds.
